          <toggle-section open="false">
            <h3>Video transcript </h3>
            <p><strong>Chris:</strong> Software should only be released after it has gone through a proper process of development, testing and bug fixing. Testing is a critical component of the development process looking at areas such as ‘performance’, ‘stability’ and ‘error handling’ by setting up test scenarios under controlled conditions and assessing the results.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> There are generally four recognized levels of testing, ‘unit or component testing’, ‘integration testing’, ‘system testing’, and ‘acceptance testing’.</p>
<p>Let’s look at these more closely.</p>
<p><strong>Chris:</strong> Unit Testing is the first level, where specific lines of code, distinct functionalities, and desired procedures are isolated and tested. These are termed ‘software units’ because they are combined to make up the software but are also referred to as ‘components’ of the software.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> For example, a line of code in a “calculator software” can have the function of implementing ‘+’ “addition,” while another line of code implements ‘x’ “multiplication,” etc. These and other functions of the calculator software must be individually tested, to ensure that the calculator operates flawlessly.</p>
<p><strong>Chris:</strong> Integration Testing involves combining all the components that make up the software, and testing ‘everything as a whole’, instead of individually, as in unit testing. From this level, tests can be split into ‘functional’ and ‘non-functional’ types which we will look at further later in this session.</p>
<p>So why is integration testing important?</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> A large team of developers working on a project will be responsible for writing different parts of the code, which will require testing to ensure that it is correct. By testing, we can identify and correct faults such as ‘inter-working defects’, ‘simultaneous operation defects’, ‘parallel operation defects’ amongst others.</p>
<p>‘Integration testing’ ascertains how well the units of code work together, and the condition of the interfaces between each of them.</p>
<p>Sometimes, a line of code may work properly when tested alone, however, when combined with other lines of code to achieve the desired function, an error may occur, indicating that the integration of one or more lines of code into the rest was unsuccessful.</p>
<p><strong>Chris:</strong> System testing is carried out to verify that software operates as required and is compatible with operating systems. It tests both the technical and business logic of the software using ‘functional tests’ on the various ‘functions’ of a system, and ‘non-functional tests’ on how those work. There have been cases where the Android version of an application may function correctly, but the iOS version has issues.</p>
<p>All the software’s completed versions are tested on all the possible operating systems, to ensure all platform functionality. A functional test may check if a login feature responds when the user enters a password, and a non-functional test may check how long it takes to log in after password entry.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> System testing can expose such errors and allow developers to detect and correct them before launch. It also looks at how good the design of the software is. The internal code may work fine, but the outward design might have some operational issues.</p>
<p>The overall behaviour of the software is another aspect that system testing helps to authenticate. It may be well developed but still malfunctions, due to the different system specifications. System testing helps identify any abnormal behaviour and helps organisations to outline the software’s best system specifications.</p>
<p><strong>Chris:</strong> Acceptance Testing also referred to as User Acceptance Testing is similar to the system testing, but the test is carried out by some selected end-users. This stage determines if the software is finally ready to be launched to the general public.</p>
<p>The selected users give their various opinions about the operation of the software letting the organization know if it meets their diverse requirements or how it can be improved upon. Organisations may conduct acceptance testing using their own specifics, but ideally users will be selected from a range of demographics, different operating systems, and past experiences of using similar software.</p>
<p>There are two types of ‘Acceptance Testing’ these are ‘Alpha and Beta Testing’.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> Alpha testing is carried out by selected users, alongside an internal team of developers who control the testing environment aiming to mimic realistic conditions during testing. The selected users are given access to the developer’s platform and allowed to use and test the developed software or application. During ‘alpha testing’, minor errors can still be found and fixed.</p>
<p><strong>Chris:</strong> Beta testing is carried out by selected users on their own devices and operating systems. Here, the finished software is sent to the selected users, to use and test it over time and then provide feedback. At the ‘beta level’, the software is expected to run perfectly, and meet the end user’s needs.</p>

