              <h3>Black box and white box testing</h3>
<p>You may have heard the terms <strong>black box testing</strong> and <strong>white box testing</strong>, so what exactly are these? Lets take a look at this in more detail.</p>

              <!-- sections.njk VIDEO! -->
              
              
              
      <div class="l-video-container">
        <video poster="~/images/svg/poster.svg" controls preload="metadata" aria-labelledby="watch" width="1000"
          data-base="cp3_u4_s1_p1_black_white_box_testing" 
          data-ext=".mp4" 
          data-multi="false"
          data-trans="cp3_u4_s1_p1_black_white_box_testing undefined">
          <source src="https://kpcontent.blob.core.windows.net/$web/resources/v2l/720/cp3_u4_s1_p1_black_white_box_testing.mp4" type="video/mp4">
          <track default label="English" kind="captions" srclang="en" src="~/videos/captions/vtt/cp3_u4_s1_p1_black_white_box_testing.vtt">
          <p>Sorry, your browser doesn't support embedded videos</p>
        </video>
      </div>
      
          <toggle-section open="false">
            <h3>Video transcript </h3>
            <p>In the short video where we will look at black box testing and white box testing. So what is black box testing? Well, a good way to think about black box testing from layman's terms is to imagine that you've been asked to sit in your car and test the, the lights, the indicators, and the electric windows.</p>
<p>So what you do? You get in the car, you switch the ignition on and you will press a button to see whether the windows go up and down. You will flick a switch to see whether the indicators work, and then flick another switch to see whether the lights come on or not. You do not need to understand the electronics behind that or, or, or what actually is going on under the hood to make the lights come on or make the windows go up and down or make the indicators work. All you need to do is you need to be able to test those by pressing a button or flicking a switch. So as you can see, anybody could carry out those tests. So black box testing in programming is exactly the same, what you have is you have a software product, which is on a black box. Which means that you cannot see inside the box, so you don't know what's in there, so you don't understand, or you can't see the actual code behind the application or the element of an application that you are testing. What you are doing is you are just looking to see that for a given input, you will get a specific output of the required output.</p>
<p>It may be several outputs, but.ultimately, that that is all you are testing, if you input specific information or you press a certain Key or input a certain piece of text or do something, do you get the output that it should give?</p>
<p>There's various types of black box testing, but these can be broken down, the three major ones are, functional testing, so that's actually just testing the functional requirements of the, the system so does every, does it do what it says, does it work well? Does it, does everything happen the way it should?</p>
<p>Then you've got the non-functional testing. Now this obviously still relates to the software, but it's all about the, the performance of the software, the scalability of the software, the usability of the software. So rather than whether do you get the required output for a given input, you also have that element of does it work as fast as it should?</p>
<p>Does it take 10 minutes for you to go? When you input into information, does it take 10 minutes for you then to get into your account, or do you get other errors and scalability if you've got, if, if the software needs to be used across multiple platforms or you know, improved or if it's designed that you've got, you know, initially thought there'd be 5,000 users, going to be 10,000 users, or you need to add bolt ons, can it be scaled up and the usability feature so is it able to be used effectively for what it was designed for?</p>
<p>Then you have the regression testing, so regression testing is if you found any problems in the functional and the non-functional testing it then goes back to the developers or the coders who will then carry out fixes and you will then do the testing, the regression testing.</p>
<p>So that will make sure that any of the changes haven't, you know, they've resolved a problem, but they haven't affected the code that was there originally. So it may be that you get one error and then they go away and fix it, but they fixed it in a certain way and you still get even more errors because it, it isn't working in relation to the, the code that was already there.</p>
<p>So an example of black box testing, so testing the login functionality of a login screen. I'm, I'm, I'm sure you've all seen this type of login screen before where you have to give a username and a password. So this is a good example of black box testing because it's simply saying that you would be required to input a correct username and password, and then you would be taken to your account or a specific page on a website.</p>
<p>But, as well as that, you'll also be testing so you know what would happen if you input an incorrect username and an incorrect password, you should be receiving a message on the screen telling you that and it may be also that if you input your username and password incorrectly three times you may be locked out and then you have to go through to another screen to complete a a validation process. So this is an example of black box testing you will test out the functionality, does it work? No, goes back to the developer, does it do what it says on the box? Yes, everything that I've tested is working.</p>
<p>So what about the advantages and disadvantages of black box testing?</p>
<p>So one of the things there is code access is not required so you don't need to see the code you are just testing the actual functionality, if you look back to the example of the the login page, all you need to see is that login page, input the information. You don't need to see the code behind that, and it also, the second one there separates a user's perspective from the developer's perspective.</p>
<p>So that's important because the developer has been, um, you know taking part in the building and the designing and the writing of the code and, so he's got an implicit, um, look and feel and he, he, he understands that he's looking for certain things. Whereas it's a case of that as the black box tester, you've got a defined role that you simply need to test the functionality of the software, so it gives that definition of rules. You can test the application with no prior knowledge of implementation programming, so as I mentioned earlier, anyone can do that you don't need to know anything prior to actually carrying out the test, and it's well suited and efficient for large code segments. So when you think about, um, you, you're gonna be testing an actual element within an application.</p>
<p>So as we say, the the login page, there'll be an awful lot of code behind that there'll be, could be a thousand units of code that are, you know, to get you to that point, so it, it is a large piece of code that you are testing and rather than sort of unit testing so it's very, very efficient and very well suited to testing that.</p>
<p>But what about the disadvantages? So one of the disadvantages that it's limited coverage, so only a specific number of tests can be performed. So you know, you've got three tests to perform do you get in when you enter your information into the login screen do you get in? You don't get in, and what happens after three?</p>
<p>Those are the only three tests that you could carry out. Um, so that comes then to the next one, which is blind coverage so you can't test or target specific code segments or error prone areas, whereas somebody who was familiar with this type of application or element within an application may know that this certain piece, um, is, is prone to errors or prone to bugs, and you wouldn't know anything about that. The test cases are difficult to design, which is true because you have to, um, there's an awful lot going on in the background. So the, you know, when you things take more time, um, it takes more costs, there's more people involved, and the inefficient testing, so the tester only has limited knowledge about an application. So it, it is a case of if you don't know that much about an application, you can only do certain tests. An example, there would be the the login page, which is quite a straightforward one, but there will be other more complicated tests to carry out. So that's the advantages and disadvantages of of black box testing.</p>
<p>So then we'll come onto white box testing. So again, as you can see, we've got a picture here, but this time we've got a picture of a mechanic as opposed to somebody sitting in a car. The reason being is that when you're doing white box testing, so if this gentleman was now testing the, the lights and the indicators and the windows, he would be able to test them under the hood, he would be able to look at the fundamental electronics and the systems within the car that make those features or functions work. So if it's not working, he knows, he can see why it's not working as opposed to the, the, the user who would only be able to flick a switch or press a button, this person would be able to go under the hood and, and look at things in more detail and more depth.</p>
<p>So as opposed to black box testing where you have no knowledge of the underlying code, in white box testing you have access to all aspects of the software. So you would be able to see the code, you'd be able to see everything that's involved or everything that makes up that application, which is why we have in white box testing, uh, a nice white box with the lid open so everything is transparent, everything is visible.</p>
<p>So why do you perform white box testing? It allows you to find internal security holes. You can see broken or poorly structured, paths in the coding, the flow of the inputs through the code, the expected outputs, the functionality of conditional loops, as you can see, all of the things here are, are elements that you would need to have a good understanding of the, of, of programming in general, and also the, the code that has been written and everything about the actual, um, application.</p>
<p>Again, there are advantages and disadvantages to white box testing. So one of the advantages that you can spot errors and problems more quickly because you're, you tend to be testing smaller elements of code. Extra lines of code can be removed, which can bring in hidden defects so again, because it's all about efficiency, you know the code, I can look at there and he can say, right, well, we don't need that so we can, we can remove those elements of, of code, find hidden bugs more efficiently, um, and allow for greater stability, again, you know, these people who will be testing it in white box testers will be very experienced, uh, programmers and testers, and they will know what to look for.and it also helps in optimizing the code and optimize code tends to be more efficient and runs faster.</p>
<p>But there are also disadvantages, so the higher degree of complexity involved. Um, so due to the detailed knowledge, so what, what you're gonna think about there is you can't just ask any Tom, Dick, or Harry to carry out the test they need to be somebody who has very um, high level of experience and understanding of the code and the programming language that is being used, and that brings onto the next one where that because skilled testers are needed, it's going to cost more money, the costs are increased, it costs far more to employ a white box tester who has all of the skills and knowledge and understanding of a variety of different programming language than it does to um, ask somebody who's just sitting in an office and saying, can you just test this for me please? So, again, you've also got the, the next one it requires tools that have tighter integration with the system being tested which, does pose a risk of system performance being affected because the tools that you're using to test it are very, can be memory intensive and system, you know, that can drain the system they can also cause errors or problems and, which would not give you the, the results that you would expect if you were just doing the, the end product testing, which is the black box testing, and no matter what you're testing it, the last one there, it's impossible to look into every nook and cranny to find out the hidden errors that may be creating problems so you can't test everything so it is a case of that, that are disadvantages to that.</p>
<p>So which one would you. So you've got your black box testing. So this one ensures that all the different software modules work well at a system level, so after they've been integrated, so you've got several components that plug them all together, you're testing them and does everything work as expected or the white box testing?</p>
<p>So again, they both have importance so this one, you know, the white box is important to catch defects at the, the unit level, which is an important part of testing as we, you know, you have looked at and we'll look at as you work through this unit and there's no really right or wrong you, you will find that both of these will be utilized. Um, but it's important to understand what each of them does and when each of them will be used. So just as a little test, um, I'd like you to carry out some research because this will be relevant to your assessment as well. There are some other different types of testing, so the static testing, dynamic testing, smoke testing, sanity testing, interface testing.</p>
<p>What I'd like you to do is carry out some research on each of those types of testing and one of the things there is you've all talked about the, the, the software development life cycle, but the, the software testing life cycle is also important. So these are all things that will or may or may not happen within that software development life cycle.</p>
<p>So it'd be interesting to see if you can carry out some research, make some notes on these as it will help you within your assessment. Just so you can get a better handle on the various types of testing this isn't the, you know, every type of testing out there there's an unlimited amount of tests that can be, um, done and new ones are being developed all the time but you will find it useful if you carry out a bit of research so you understand these different types of testing.</p>

